  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 302 PM CDT Fri Sep 11 2026\n\nAn upper shortwave trough is moving into the Lower Mississippi \nValley with upper troughing over southern Canada into the Northern \nPlains. Zonal flow aloft continues over much of the western CONUS. \nAt the surface, a low is over South Dakota with a cold front \nextending into Wyoming. In our area, breezy southerly winds \ncontinue with highs in the 80s to low 90s. As we move into \ntonight, the cold front will push south into Nebraska likely \ninitiating storms in southern Nebraska. Its possible for this \nactivity to drift south into Kansas early Saturday morning. \nDeterministic models continue to be consistent with this \nsolution compared to short-term guidance with some CAMs showing \nshowers across portions of our area. Severe weather is unlikely \nwith this activity and confidence remains low on storm chances \novernight into Saturday morning. \n\nFor Saturday, recent guidance has trended south with the frontal \nboundary, likely stalling out along Highway 50. High temperatures \nare once again forecast for the upper 80s to the lower 90s. By late \nafternoon, diurnal heating will lead to plenty of instability \n(greater than 2500 J/kg) developing along and south of the boundary, \nmaking another chance for storms possible late Saturday afternoon \ninto Saturday night. Severe storms are likely with this activity \nwith the main hazards being large hail and damaging winds. Steep mid-\nlevel lapse rates combined with 40 kts of effective shear will be \nsufficient for producing up to half dollar size hail. Damaging wind \ngusts are also possible given steep low-level lapse rates with DCAPE \nvalues over 1300 J/kg. Given cloud bases will be pretty elevated \ncombined with weak low-level shear, the threat for tornadoes will \nstay extremely low. Storm chances may linger into Saturday night and \nearly Sunday morning as the nocturnal LLJ ramps up. Storm chances \nwill decrease after Sunday morning as low to mid-level WAA decreases \nacross the area. Mostly dry conditions are forecast for Sunday \nafternoon through Sunday night. Lee surface troughing will increase \nresulting in breezy southerly winds across the area. High \ntemperatures will be in 90s with the warmest readings expected in \nsouth-central KS. Heat indices will be around 100 Sunday afternoon \ninto the evening. \n\nFor Monday and beyond, an upper trough over the northwestern US will \npropagate northeast towards the Northern Plains by Monday afternoon. \nAs this trough continues to slide across the Northern Plains, it \nwill push a cold front south into Nebraska by Monday afternoon. Lee \ntroughing will remain in place across Kansas with breezy southerly \nwinds anticipated. Another hot day is in store for Monday as highs \nonce again rise into the upper 90s. This cold front will continue to \npush south, likely allowing showers and storms to develop along it, \nincreasing chances for Monday night into Tuesday. By late Tuesday \nafternoon, the front will likely make it to central Kansas. This \nwill result in highs ranging from the 70s in far northern Kansas to \nthe 90s in far southern Kansas. Following this front, highs are \nforecast to return to the 80s and low 90s for Wednesday and \nThursday. Periodic rain chances are possible for beyond Tuesday as \nguidance suggests additional shortwaves to slide across the region. \nStay tuned!",
